tourHistory:
  - >-
    The 12th annual Vans Warped Tour hit Kansas during the absolute peak of
    mid-2000s punk, emo, and post-hardcore crossover. Sprawling across multiple
    parking-lot and amphitheater stages in the summer heat, the 2006 bill paired
    legacy punk institutions like NOFX and Joan Jett with a wave of heavy bands
    breaking straight onto the Billboard upper tier.
  - >-
    This stop landed right around June 20, 2006 — the exact release date of
    Underoath’s landmark album Define the Great Line — turning an already
    stacked summer festival into a snapshot of the scene at maximum velocity.
bandNotes:
  - band: Underoath
    note: >-
      Released Define the Great Line on June 20, 2006 — literally the week of
      this show — debuting at #2 on the Billboard 200 and cementing their status
      as the biggest heavy band on the tour.
  - band: Rise Against
    note: >-
      Days away from releasing their breakout major-label classic The Sufferer &
      the Witness (July 4, 2006), previewing new material to packed main-stage
      crowds.
  - band: From First to Last
    note: >-
      Touring their Ross Robinson-produced second album Heroine (March 2006) in
      what turned out to be vocalist Sonny Moore’s final Warped Tour before
      departing in early 2007 and later emerging as Skrillex.
  - band: Thursday
    note: >-
      Supporting A City by the Light Divided (May 2006), expanding their
      post-hardcore sound with producer Dave Fridmann.
  - band: NOFX
    note: >-
      Riding the April 2006 release of Wolves in Wolves’ Clothing as the tour’s
      veteran punk anchors.
  - band: Every Time I Die
    note: >-
      Touring their Ferret Music breakthrough Gutter Phenomenon (2005),
      sharpening the Southern-fried metallic hardcore attack that made them
      Warped staples.
  - band: Emery
    note: >-
      Coming off their 2005 breakout The Question, carrying dual-vocal
      post-hardcore anthems across the festival stages.
  - band: Motion City Soundtrack & Saves the Day
    note: >-
      Motion City Soundtrack was riding the Mark Hoppus-produced Commit This to
      Memory (2005), while Saves the Day had just released Sound the Alarm
      (April 2006).
